Let a provider's property map write any field of the site's user type, including the fields of the behaviors it enables, instead of only `fullname`, `home_page`, `description` and `location`. A claim that already has the field's shape, such as a list of links for a list field, is written as the provider sent it, after the field validates it; a value the field refuses is logged and skipped. The control panel's picker, the registry and a profile's `registry.xml` accept the same fields, so a profile that maps a behavior's field enables the behavior first. The login, the userid, membership, roles, addresses and the portrait are still never written by a map. A principal document now also carries every field a map may write beyond those four, under an optional `fields` key in the JSON shape `plone.restapi` uses, so an export and import between sites keeps them; a field the importing site's user type lacks is reported and skipped. @ericof
